    "es": "La única sangre con la que se van a firmar estos contratos es con la que me salga al cortarme intentando abrir la maldita caja del CD.",
    "en": "The only blood these contracts are signed in is from me cutting my hand trying to open the goddamn CD case.",
    "noteEn": "The Spanish archive localizes Faust as Fausto and closely translates the official title text's blood-contract and difficult-CD-case joke. No source metadata anomaly was found."
  },
  "cardDefinitions": [
    {
      "id": "word-mortal--human",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "mortal",
      "answerEn": "mortal; human being",
      "noteEn": "As a noun, mortal means a human being viewed as someone who will eventually die. It can appear with an article, as in el mortal, or as a direct form of address without one.",
      "example": { "es": "Los mortales no viven para siempre.", "en": "Mortals do not live forever." },
      "tags": ["word", "person", "noun", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-te--recipient",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "te",
      "answerEn": "to you; for you (one familiar person)",
      "noteEn": "Te can identify one familiar listener as the person who receives something or benefits from an action. It normally comes before a verb that has changed for the person and time.",
      "example": { "es": "Te llevo un café.", "en": "I am bringing you a coffee." },
      "tags": ["word", "recipient", "pronoun", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-traigo",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "traigo",
      "answerEn": "I bring; I am bringing",
      "noteEn": "Traigo is the present form of traer used when the speaker says yo. The middle of the verb changes: traer becomes traigo.",
      "example": { "es": "Traigo pan para la cena.", "en": "I am bringing bread for dinner." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb", "traer", "A1"]
    },
    { "id": "word-oferta", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "oferta", "answerEn": "offer; deal", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "noun", "A1"] },
    {
      "id": "word-lee--read-command",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "lee",
      "answerEn": "read (informal singular command)",
      "noteEn": "Lee is the positive command from leer used when telling one familiar person to read.",
      "example": { "es": "Lee este mensaje.", "en": "Read this message." },
      "tags": ["word", "command", "leer", "A1"]
    },
    { "id": "word-habitación", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "habitación", "answerEn": "room", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "place", "noun", "A1"] },
    {
      "id": "word-acepta--agrees",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "acepta",
      "answerEn": "you agree; you accept (formal singular)",
      "noteEn": "Acepta is the present form of aceptar used for usted, the polite or formal way to address one person. The same written form can also mean “he accepts” or “she accepts.”",
      "example": { "es": "Usted acepta las condiciones.", "en": "You agree to the conditions." },
      "tags": ["word", "agreement", "formal address", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-propia",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "propia",
      "answerEn": "own (feminine singular)",
      "noteEn": "Propia emphasizes that something belongs to the person already identified. It takes the feminine singular form when it describes a feminine singular noun.",
      "example": { "es": "Clara tomó su propia decisión.", "en": "Clara made her own decision." },
      "tags": ["word", "possession", "agreement", "A2"]
    },
    { "id": "word-negociar", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "negociar", "answerEn": "to negotiate", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "verb", "A2"] },
    {
      "id": "word-ella--prepositional-inanimate",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "ella",
      "answerEn": "it (referring to a feminine thing after a preposition)",
      "noteEn": "After a preposition such as en, con, or de, ella can refer back to a feminine singular thing as well as to a woman. English normally uses “it” for the thing.",
      "example": { "es": "La caja está vacía; no hay nada en ella.", "en": "The box is empty; there is nothing in it." },
      "tags": ["word", "pronoun", "inanimate reference", "A2"]
    },
    { "id": "word-reside", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "reside", "answerEn": "lives; resides", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "verb", "A2"] },
    { "id": "word-mefistófeles", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "mefistófeles", "answerEn": "Mephistopheles", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "proper name", "literature"] },
    {
      "id": "word-se--encontrarse-con",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "se",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; helps encontrarse con mean ‘to meet’ or ‘encounter’",
      "noteEn": "Encontrarse uses a short matching word such as me, te, se, nos, or os. Se is the form used with one third-person subject such as Ana or Mefistófeles.",
      "example": { "es": "Ana se encuentra con un vecino.", "en": "Ana runs into a neighbor." },
      "tags": ["word", "pronoun", "encontrarse con", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-encuentra--encounters",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "encuentra",
      "answerEn": "meets; encounters (with se and con)",
      "noteEn": "Encuentra is the present form used for one third-person subject. With se before it and con after it, se encuentra con describes meeting or coming across someone or something.",
      "example": { "es": "Leo se encuentra con Marta.", "en": "Leo meets Marta." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb", "encounter", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-con--encontrarse-complement",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "con",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word here; required before who or what is encountered",
      "noteEn": "Spanish keeps con in encontrarse con alguien or algo. Natural English normally says “meet someone” or “encounter something” without a separate word for con.",
      "example": { "es": "Me encontré con Julia.", "en": "I ran into Julia." },
      "tags": ["word", "preposition", "encontrarse con", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-aceptar-infinitive",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "aceptar + verbo en forma de diccionario",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone accepts or agrees to do an action?",
      "answerEn": "Use a form of aceptar followed directly by the action verb in its unchanged dictionary form.",
      "noteEn": "The plus sign in the prompt shows the order of the two pieces. The dictionary form is the unchanged verb ending in -ar, -er, or -ir. Aceptar changes for the person and time, while the action verb stays unchanged: acepto ayudar, acepta esperar, aceptaron pagar. Do not insert a or de between them.",
      "example": { "es": "Lucía acepta ayudar.", "en": "Lucía agrees to help." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "agreement", "verb complement", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-encontrarse-con",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "encontrarse con alguien o algo",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone meets, encounters, or runs into a person or thing?",
      "answerEn": "Use the matching form of encontrarse, then con and the person or thing encountered.",
      "noteEn": "Encontrarse includes a short word that matches the person doing the meeting: me encuentro, te encuentras, se encuentra, nos encontramos, or se encuentran. Con stays before the person or thing encountered. This differs from encontrarse en, which describes where something is located, and from encontrar without the matching short word, which commonly means “to find.”",
      "example": { "es": "Marta se encuentra con un amigo.", "en": "Marta runs into a friend." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "meeting", "encounter", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "concept-faust-eula-soul-contract-parody",
      "kind": "concept",
      "promptEs": "Fausto, Mefistófeles y una E.U.L.A.",
      "questionEn": "What makes the soul-bargain scene a parody of a software agreement?",
      "answerEn": "Mephistopheles traditionally bargains for Faust's soul; the comic makes him lose that leverage to an end-user software license agreement.",
      "noteEn": "In the Faust legend, Mephistopheles offers knowledge or power in exchange for Faust's soul. E.U.L.A. is the English abbreviation for End User License Agreement, the terms a software user accepts. The sign turns those routine terms into a prior contract that blocks the demon's negotiation.",
      "tags": ["concept", "Faust", "Mephistopheles", "EULA", "literary parody"]
    }
  ],
